Dear Fabbaloo Editors,

Solidon3D 0.3.0 has been released. It is local desktop software for adapting
downloaded 3D-printing models and constructing printable parts from named
dimensions, sketches and tested components. It is meant to bridge “model
downloaded” and “part fits,” not replace a full CAD system.

The development story is unusually substantial for the version number: the
small 0.1 demo has grown into a coherent workflow through 220 curated changes
that users can actually see.

The clearest before-and-after example is an imported STL with the wrong hole.
Solidon now recognises the cylindrical feature, keeps its original position
and direction, and changes the diameter as a non-destructive operation. The
user can then place a standards-based insert or fastener, calibrate the fit
with a printed gauge, and revise any of those decisions later.

The same operation history can now become a reusable parametric part without
writing code. Its fields, preview and valid range are stored as a safe recipe
of registered operations, so the part can travel inside a project without
carrying executable source.

Other additions since 0.1 include in-viewport sketching, matching printable
nuts and screws, bearing seats, per-spool process data in 3MF, and full
Windows, macOS and Linux packaging. The optional LLM layer can request those
same operations, but it never computes geometry or executes generated code.

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Changelog: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

